Everything you fix here is a sentence no item has to write. The items are to read as a textbook reads -- standard terms, no typeclass or universe bookkeeping, no "let $I$ be an index type" -- and that is only possible if the conventions carry the ambient objects, the structure they bear, the letters, the notation, the coercions and the side conditions the library discharges inside its statements. Write them so that a drafter who follows `informal/STYLE.md` never has to repeat one.

## Read first
`informal/items.json` (approved: its ids are the printed numbers), `informal/declarations.json`, `informal/STYLE.md`, the templates you replace (`informal/notation.sty`, `informal/sections/00-conventions.tex`, `informal/STYLE-project.md`), and the library's source under `{{library_src}}/`.

## Write
1. `informal/hypotheses.json`: `{"schema": "formal-companion/informalize-hypotheses", "schema_version": 1, "hypotheses": [...]}`, one row per standing hypothesis the key declarations bind: `key` (letters, digits, `-`), `macro` (`\hypSomething`), and `binder_head` (the head constant of the hypothesis's printed type) or `binder_type` (a Python regular expression the whole printed type matches, spaces collapsed). An empty list when there is none.
2. `informal/notation.sty`: one `\fcdefhyp{\macro}{key}{words}` per row of hypotheses.json (the words a statement prints for the hypothesis: the shortest wording that says the binder and nothing more, the standard term where one says exactly it), and the notation the sections use, defined with `\newcommand` or `\DeclareMathOperator`; each letter with one meaning.
3. `informal/sections/00-conventions.tex`: unnumbered prose fixing the objects, the letters and their one meaning each, the standing hypotheses and their macros, and every junk value the items rely on (what Lean returns where the mathematics is undefined), from the library's docstrings and statements, in your own words. Say the standard term the text uses for each notion, and state once any equivalence the items then rely on (that a set is a neighborhood of each of its points exactly when its interior contains them, say), so that a statement may use the term instead of spelling the binder out. Mark each notion with the definition of the library it is, as `informal/STYLE.md` ("The conventions section") says: `\fcnotion{<constant>}{<the words as printed>}`, once, where the conventions introduce it. The constant is a name `declarations.json` carries -- an entry of its `declarations`, or a row of its `references` for a definition or class of a library underneath -- copied from that file and never written from memory. **Print no Lean name and no link**: no `\texttt`, no `\verb`, no `\href`, no `\url`, no URL, here or anywhere in `informal/`. The annotations carry the name and the link; the text carries the mathematics.
4. `informal/STYLE-project.md`: the project's half of the style rules (the hypotheses, the letters, the notation, the standard terms and the constants that mark them, what the conventions carry so that no item repeats it, and how the reference work is cited).

